const shankaraSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation','Nonduality argued through teaching and commentary',[
    'Śaṅkara is the most authoritative early systematizer of Advaita Vedānta, but he did not invent Vedānta or a timeless “Hindu monism.” His surviving works enter an older conversation about the Upaniṣads, the Bhagavad Gītā, and the Brahma Sūtra. They argue that the deepest self is not a bounded owner of experience but nondual, self-revealing consciousness identical with brahman. That proposal is metaphysical, epistemological, and practical: it asks what is ultimately real, how error makes plurality seem final, and how words can remove a mistake about what the listener already is.',
    'Calling this “everything is one” loses the method. Śaṅkara distinguishes the ordinary world in which persons deliberate, act, worship, and learn from the liberating standpoint in which no second reality limits brahman. He does not ask beginners to deny tables, grief, duty, or teachers. He asks how any object, mental state, or identity becomes known, then directs attention toward the consciousness presupposed by knowing. The article therefore treats Advaita as a disciplined commentarial argument, not a mood of cosmic togetherness or a claim that an individual ego controls reality.'
  ],['ramanuja','madhva'],['Upadeśasāhasrī','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya'],['vedanta','metaphysics','philosophy-of-mind']),
  section('life-history','A historical author behind later sacred biographies',[
    'The familiar lifespan 788–820 CE is a traditional convention, not secure documentary precision. Current scholarship often places Śaṅkara in the early eighth century and entertains other ranges. His own works provide little ordinary biography. Śaṅkaravijaya narratives written centuries later describe birth at Kālaḍi, early renunciation, journeys across India, debates, disciples, monastery foundations, and death at thirty-two. Those stories are indispensable evidence for how communities remembered an exemplary teacher, but they cannot be combined uncritically into a contemporary itinerary.',
    'The same distinction applies to institutional memory. Daśanāmī renouncers and several maṭhas trace authority to Śaṅkara, and these living lineages deserve description in their own terms. Historical research nevertheless asks when orders, titles, genealogies, and foundation stories took their transmitted forms. Even Śaṅkara’s Śaiva or Vaiṣṇava orientation remains debated. A careful profile can say that tradition reveres him as an ācārya, that later institutions claim him, and that his works reveal a renunciant Vedāntin, without treating every conquest story, miracle, or pan-Indian foundation as verified biography.'
  ],[],['Śaṅkaravijayas']),
  section('corpus','The secure corpus and the problem of attribution',[
    'Śaṅkara’s historical identity rests above all on commentaries. Scholars generally accept the Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya, commentaries on the Bhagavad Gītā and principal Upaniṣads, and the independent Upadeśasāhasrī, while particular works and passages still receive philological scrutiny. The commentaries respond to different source texts and opponents; they do not read like chapters of one modern treatise. Their agreement must be reconstructed without erasing changes of genre, pedagogical situation, or the difference between explaining a verse and stating an independent thesis.',
    'Hundreds of hymns, manuals, and short treatises circulate under Śaṅkara’s name. Some are important Advaita texts even when later authorship is probable; traditional attribution records authority and reception as well as modern authorship. The editorial safeguard is simple: never cite Vivekacūḍāmaṇi, Ātma-bodha, or a devotional hymn as unquestioned evidence for the eighth-century philosopher. This profile centers securely attributed works and labels wider Śaṅkara corpora as reception history. That protects both historical inquiry and the genuine creativity of later Advaitins who wrote under an authoritative name or title.'
  ],[],['Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya','Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya','Upadeśasāhasrī']),
  section('superimposition','Superimposition and beginningless ignorance',[
    'Śaṅkara opens the Brahma Sūtra commentary by describing superimposition: features of a subject and object are transferred onto one another. We say “I am tall,” “I am wounded,” or “I remember,” identifying the self with body, senses, agency, and mental history; we also treat the body as a conscious “me.” This reciprocal confusion organizes ordinary life. It is beginningless in the sense that no first mistaken episode is available within experience, yet it can end through knowledge. Ignorance is therefore not mere missing information but a lived misidentification reproduced in perception, desire, and action.',
    'Later Advaita schools developed competing theories about avidyā: its locus, object, ontological status, and relation to individual minds. Those refinements should not all be projected into Śaṅkara. His pedagogical priority is removal. A rope mistaken for a snake does not require production of a new rope; appropriate knowledge cancels the false attribution. Similarly, teaching does not manufacture an infinite self. It exposes why finite predicates never exhaust the consciousness to which they appear. The analogy is limited—bondage involves a whole practical world, not one visual error—but it shows why liberation is knowledge rather than a newly created result.'
  ]),
  section('reality-causation','Brahman, appearance, and orders of reality',[
    'Brahman is nondual existence and consciousness, not one very large entity inside the universe. Śaṅkara reads Upaniṣadic statements of origin to show that effects are not independent of their cause: a pot depends on clay, while clay does not depend on that particular pot. Yet brahman does not undergo ordinary material alteration into a damaged or divided cosmos. Name and form make differentiated experience possible while remaining dependent. Later writers call this vivarta, apparent transformation, but the exact terminology and theory develop across Advaita’s history.',
    'The empirical world is thus neither sheer nothing nor a second self-sufficient reality. It is transactionally valid: fire burns, testimony teaches, injustice harms, and action bears consequences. It is sublated only by knowledge of its non-independent basis. Dream examples test our confidence that a vividly structured field must be ultimate; they do not prove that waking life is a private hallucination. “Māyā means illusion” is too blunt because māyā, avidyā, name and form, and indeterminability do different work in different passages and later systems. The safe claim is dependence without ultimate secondness.'
  ]),
  section('consciousness','Witnessing consciousness and the changing mind',[
    'Advaita distinguishes consciousness from the mind’s changing modes. Thoughts, memories, emotions, and perceptions present contents; consciousness is what makes those contents manifest. From an empirical standpoint it can be called witness, sākṣin, because waking, dreaming, and deep sleep vary while the continuity of a subject is not experienced as a series of unrelated owners. Consciousness is self-revealing: another cognition is not needed to light it up, since any proposed second act would already depend on awareness. It is not an inner spectator shaped like a subtle person watching a mental screen.',
    'From the ultimate standpoint even “witness” is relational language, because a witness implies something witnessed. Śaṅkara’s teaching provisionally separates consciousness from body and mind, then withdraws the remaining subject–object contrast. His discussion of deep sleep is philosophically contested: he takes the absence of presented objects not to be proof that consciousness disappears. Contemporary comparisons with phenomenology, panpsychism, or theories of non-intentional awareness can be illuminating, but they are reconstructions. Śaṅkara did not offer a neuroscientific hypothesis, and brahman is not a cosmic brain assembled from private streams.'
  ],[],[],['philosophy-of-mind']),
  section('language','How words can disclose what is not an object',[
    'If brahman cannot be objectified, scriptural sentences face an apparent problem. Śaṅkara treats the Upaniṣads as a means of knowledge for something unavailable to perception and inference alone. Their words initially use familiar relations—creator and world, knower and known, inner controller, person—then remove limiting conceptions. Negative expressions do not describe an empty blank. They prevent finite predicates from turning brahman into an object alongside others, while identity statements redirect the word “you” from biography and body toward consciousness.',
    '“That you are” therefore requires teaching, grammar, context, and a prepared hearer. The sentence does not say the empirical personality is omniscient, nor that two independently known objects fuse. It cancels incompatible limiting features so the underlying non-difference can be recognized. Hirst describes Śaṅkara’s method as a way of teaching because different passages lead from different starting assumptions. No isolated “great saying” functions as a password. Listening must be joined to reflective testing, and interpretation must account for passages about difference, devotion, creation, and action rather than deleting them.'
  ],[],['Chāndogya Upaniṣad Bhāṣya','Upadeśasāhasrī']),
  section('practice','Knowledge, action, devotion, and preparedness',[
    'Śaṅkara denies that ritual action and liberating knowledge combine as coordinate causes of mokṣa. Action produces or modifies a result; knowledge removes error about an already accomplished reality. This does not make ethics, devotion, meditation, or disciplined action worthless. Karma-yoga, restraint, discrimination between enduring and transient goals, dispassion, steadiness, and a desire for liberation prepare the mind to receive teaching. Worship of Īśvara and action offered without possessiveness can loosen the habits that make finite agency appear absolute.',
    'The distinction is between direct and indirect means, not between thought and life. A person whose attention is ruled by resentment, acquisitiveness, or distraction may repeat an identity formula without understanding it. Hearing the teaching (śravaṇa), reasoning through doubts (manana), and sustained contemplation (nididhyāsana) name an ordered pedagogy, though scholars debate exactly how voluntary contemplation relates to knowledge. Renunciation has a privileged place in Śaṅkara’s works, and his social and ritual assumptions are historically restrictive. Modern applications should not conceal those exclusions behind a universalized language of pure awareness.'
  ],[],['Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya']),
  section('isvara-ethics','Īśvara, moral order, and the problem of flattening devotion',[
    'At the empirical level Śaṅkara affirms Īśvara, the intelligent and material cause through which cosmic order, scripture, and karmic results are explained. The Lord is not merely an embarrassing lower truth that devotees should discard. Devotion, surrender of results, and contemplation belong to the transformative world in which teaching occurs. At the same time, nondual analysis denies that Lord and self remain two ultimate realities. Later Advaita traditions differ in how they articulate worship, grace, divine forms, and the relation between nirguṇa and saguṇa brahman.',
    'This layered account creates ethical questions. If agency and plurality are ultimately sublated, why avoid harm? Śaṅkara’s answer operates within the valid empirical order: duties, consequences, mental purification, compassion, and the recognition of self across beings discipline conduct. Yet a modern reader should not infer a complete egalitarian politics that the texts do not supply. Nonduality can motivate solidarity, but its historical social forms also carried caste, gender, and access restrictions. Philosophical potential and institutional record must be assessed together rather than allowing metaphysical unity to certify every later Advaita claim.'
  ]),
  section('opponents','Arguments among Indian schools, not “East versus West”',[
    'Śaṅkara argues with Mīmāṃsā over action and scripture, Sāṃkhya over an independent primordial matter, Nyāya and Vaiśeṣika over categories and selves, Buddhist positions over momentariness, cognition, emptiness, and idealism, and other Vedāntins over causation and difference. His labels are often polemical classifications rather than neutral surveys. “Crypto-Buddhist” is especially unhelpful as a summary: Advaita and Buddhist philosophies may share argumentative pressure points while disagreeing over self, scripture, causation, and liberation.',
    'Rāmānuja and Madhva later attack Advaita’s account of ignorance and its handling of difference. Their objections are not failures to reach Śaṅkara’s insight; they expose genuine costs in explaining error, devotion, personal identity, and a real world. Post-Śaṅkara Advaitins including Padmapāda, Sureśvara, Vācaspati Miśra, Prakāśātman, and Madhusūdana Sarasvatī then build divergent explanatory systems and answer new opponents. The tradition’s influence comes through this contested commentary, not the passive repetition of one founder’s doctrine. Reading the objections is part of understanding Advaita.'
  ],['ramanuja','madhva']),
  section('reception','From early Advaita to modern global Vedānta',[
    'Śaṅkara’s authority grew through commentaries, subcommentaries, teaching lineages, renouncer institutions, and later narratives of philosophical conquest. Bhāmatī and Vivaraṇa classifications organize some later debates but do not reduce Advaita to two uniform camps. Medieval and early-modern thinkers refined theories of ignorance, cognition, language, and liberation while meeting Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, Dvaita, and Viśiṣṭādvaita criticism. Living lineages preserve study and practice, yet their claims of continuity remain theological and institutional claims as well as subjects of historical research.',
    'Nineteenth- and twentieth-century teachers rearticulated Advaita amid colonial rule, print, nationalism, missionary encounter, and global religious markets. Vivekananda’s practical and universal Vedānta, Ramana Maharshi’s inquiry, monastic teaching organizations, and contemporary wellness discourse are significant receptions, not transparent replicas of an eighth-century program. “All religions say the same thing” should not be placed in Śaṅkara’s mouth. Comparison is strongest when it identifies the new ethical and political work of a modern reconstruction while leaving classical disputes visible.'
  ]),
  section('reading-path','A reading path that preserves argument and uncertainty',[
    'Begin with selected Upaniṣadic dialogues in a modern scholarly translation, then read the Bhagavad Gītā while tracking action, knowledge, devotion, and renunciation. Approach Śaṅkara through the Upadeśasāhasrī’s prose teaching and selected secure commentaries rather than a collection of inspirational quotations. In the Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya, study the introductory superimposition passage, 1.1.1 on inquiry, and carefully guided selections on causation, self, and liberation. Compare multiple translations whenever an argument depends on a technical term.',
    'Next read a specialist account of Śaṅkara’s pedagogy and Dalal’s source-rich overview. Pair them with one later Advaita dispute and with Rāmānuja’s critique, asking what each side counts as knowledge, error, and adequate interpretation. Keep three columns in your notes: secure eighth-century attribution, later Advaita development, and modern reception. That simple practice prevents famous later works and universalist slogans from swallowing the historical thinker. The reward is a harder but more coherent philosophy in which nonduality, language, disciplined reasoning, and a lived path belong together. It also makes disagreement productive: the test is not whether a later reader feels nondual, but whether the proposed account can explain experience, correction, scriptural communication, devotion, and liberation without borrowing its conclusion.'
  ],['ramanuja'],['Upadeśasāhasrī','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya','Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya'])
];

const ramanujaSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation','A qualified whole of God, selves, and world',[
    'Rāmānuja is the great systematizer of Viśiṣṭādvaita Vedānta and a principal ācārya of the Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition. He argues that brahman is the personal Lord Nārāyaṇa, inseparable from Śrī, possessing unlimited auspicious qualities. Finite selves and the material world are real, yet they exist in complete dependence on brahman. Nonduality therefore names one inclusive, internally differentiated reality, not a bare consciousness from which plurality finally disappears. “Qualified nondualism” is serviceable shorthand, but “nondualism of the differentiated” better preserves his refusal to choose between unity and real distinction.',
    'The body–self analogy gives this account its shape. Souls and matter form the body of brahman because they are supported, governed, and used for divine purposes; brahman is their inner self. A body is not identical with its indwelling self in every respect, so the analogy does not absorb persons into God. Nor is the world an optional addition that completes a deficient deity. Rāmānuja’s central question is how one reality can include genuine subjects, objects, values, action, devotion, and grace without surrendering divine unity.'
  ],['shankara','madhva'],['Śrī Bhāṣya','Vedārthasaṅgraha'],['vedanta','philosophy-of-religion','metaphysics']),
  section('history-memory','Chronology, community memory, and the limits of biography',[
    'Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition gives Rāmānuja the lifespan 1017–1137, an extraordinary 120 years; scholarship has also proposed 1077–1157 and other adjustments. The Atlas retains the traditional dates as labeled anchors rather than secure precision. Later biographies narrate his birth at Śrīperumbūdūr, studies in Kāñcīpuram, break with Yādavaprakāśa, initiation, renunciation, debates, temple reforms, exile, return, and death at Śrīraṅgam. These accounts preserve sacred memory and community identity, but their vivid incidents must not be flattened into modern documentary biography.',
    'Ranjeeta Dutta shows that hagiographies, inscriptions, praise poems, and modern biographies interact in constructing remembered pasts. Stories of assassination plots, miraculous signs, royal conversion, secret teaching made public, or uniformly anti-caste reform have distinct devotional and political afterlives. They may disclose ideals of humility, accessibility, and authorized succession without proving every event. A historically responsible life centers Rāmānuja’s Sanskrit works, his South Indian Śrī Vaiṣṇava setting, inherited teachers, temple-centered devotion, and institutional influence while labeling narrative detail by evidentiary genre.'
  ]),
  section('context','Sanskrit Vedānta in a Tamil devotional world',[
    'Rāmānuja wrote within a South Indian community shaped by Sanskrit Veda and Vedānta, Tamil Āḻvār hymns, Pāñcarātra theology and ritual, temple worship, teachers such as Nāthamuni and Yāmuna, and living relations among householders, ascetics, specialists, and devotees. “Two Vedāntas” later names the Sanskrit and Tamil textual inheritances held together in Śrī Vaiṣṇava life. Rāmānuja’s own major philosophical works are Sanskrit, so one should neither call him the author of Tamil devotional theology nor detach his arguments from the ritual and poetic world that made them matter.',
    'Yāmuna is an indispensable predecessor even if hagiography’s hoped-for meeting with Rāmānuja cannot anchor a secure succession scene. Earlier theistic and difference-and-nondifference Vedānta also complicates the claim that Rāmānuja invented the first alternative to Śaṅkara. His achievement is a powerful synthesis and defense: he gives a rigorous Brahma Sūtra commentary, coordinates diverse Upaniṣadic passages, and makes devotion to a perfect personal brahman philosophically central. Tradition formation here is cumulative, multilingual, and institutional rather than the act of a solitary founder.'
  ],[],['Nālāyira Divya Prabandham','Siddhitraya']),
  section('works','A corpus of commentary, synthesis, and devotion',[
    'The Śrī Bhāṣya is Rāmānuja’s expansive Brahma Sūtra commentary and the principal architecture of his Vedānta. Its opening includes the “great prior position,” a sustained reconstruction of Advaita, followed by detailed replies about ignorance, consciousness, scripture, and reality. The Vedārthasaṅgraha organizes Upaniṣadic teaching without following one root text line by line. The Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya interprets action, knowledge, and devotion as an ordered path. Vedāntadīpa and Vedāntasāra offer more concise sūtra explanations, although audiences and relative chronology need cautious handling.',
    'Tradition also attributes three prose hymns known as the Gadyatraya and the ritual Nityagrantha. Their relation to Rāmānuja’s authorship and to the systematic works has generated debate. The devotional texts became especially important for teachings of surrender, but a page should not use them silently to settle what the Śrī Bhāṣya says about access to liberation. Secure method names the work and genre. Rāmānuja is best encountered not through a list of nine titles treated as equivalent data points, but through the distinct tasks of argument, exegesis, prayer, and practice.'
  ],[],['Śrī Bhāṣya','Vedārthasaṅgraha','Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya','Gadyatraya']),
  section('realism-consciousness','Consciousness belongs to enduring knowers',[
    'Rāmānuja attacks the Advaita claim that pure objectless consciousness is the single ultimate reality. Cognition is always awareness of something characterized in some way, and consciousness belongs to a real subject. The self is not the body, but neither is it numerically identical with brahman. It is an enduring knower and agent whose attributive consciousness, dharmabhūtajñāna, reaches objects. Bondage contracts this cognitive capacity under karma; liberation removes obstruction and permits expansive awareness without erasing the self’s distinct identity.',
    'This view supports an epistemic realism. Perception presents qualified things rather than an unintelligible bare particular later assembled by thought. Even error depends on real elements and a real knower; an illusion does not prove that cognition as such is false. Rāmānuja argues that a theory declaring all ordinary cognition infected by ignorance risks undermining the very cognition used to assert that verdict. His account is not naïve acceptance of every appearance. It asks which explanation of correction, memory, language, and successful action best preserves our ability to know a differentiated world.'
  ],[],['Śrī Bhāṣya 1.1.1']),
  section('body-self','The world as the body of brahman',[
    'The relation of body to self is defined functionally: a body is a reality supported and controlled by a self and existing for that self’s purposes. On this model, conscious selves and nonconscious matter are modes or attributes of brahman while remaining real in their own natures. Brahman is both the material and intelligent cause of the cosmos, but creation is not from absolute nothing. In a subtle state souls and matter depend on God; cosmic manifestation reorganizes their conditions without converting divine perfection into suffering or material limitation.',
    '“Panentheism” can offer comparison—all things are in God, while God exceeds them—but it is an external category and cannot replace Rāmānuja’s grammar of body, self, mode, control, and dependence. The analogy also creates a difficult ethical question: if embodied beings suffer within God’s body, how is divine goodness preserved? Rāmānuja appeals to beginningless karma, ordered consequences, divine support, and the possibility of liberation. Contemporary readers may test that theodicy, but should first reconstruct how agency and dependence differ from both independent substances and a world dismissed as appearance.'
  ]),
  section('language','Reading identity sentences without erasing difference',[
    'Vedānta schools share sentences such as “that you are” and disagree about how their words refer. Rāmānuja uses coordinate predication: different terms can designate one complex reality through different aspects. “That” indicates brahman as cosmic cause and “you” indicates the inner self of an individual; because the individual is brahman’s body, both ultimately refer to brahman, but they do not declare the finite person identical with God in every respect. Unity of reference therefore preserves internal differentiation.',
    'He joins this reading to a larger hermeneutic rule: passages describing brahman’s qualities, agency, knowledge, bliss, difference, and embodiment cannot be demoted merely because some passages emphasize unity or negation. “Without qualities” excludes defects and material limitations rather than every characteristic. The Vedārthasaṅgraha groups texts by their purport and the Śrī Bhāṣya argues against Advaita interpretations through grammar, context, common experience, and consistency. These are rival public readings of an archive, not reports of private mystical experiences that happen to use different words.'
  ],[],['Chāndogya Upaniṣad 6.8.7','Vedārthasaṅgraha']),
  section('god-sri','Nārāyaṇa, Śrī, and auspicious qualities',[
    'Rāmānuja identifies brahman with the supreme personal Lord Nārāyaṇa, characterized by knowledge, power, sovereignty, compassion, beauty, and other unlimited auspicious qualities. Negation removes imperfection; it does not leave an indeterminate absolute behind the devotional God. Śrī or Lakṣmī is inseparable from Nārāyaṇa in Śrī Vaiṣṇava theology. Sources differ over how precisely to articulate her metaphysical and mediating role, and later lineages develop the relation in distinct ways. A compact formula should not substitute for that history.',
    'Divine embodiment in temple images is therefore not an inferior concession to people unable to grasp metaphysics. The Lord’s accessibility, worship, and gracious self-presentation belong to a theology in which qualities and relations are ultimately real. Still, Rāmānuja’s philosophical works and later temple theology are not identical corpora. The article connects them through Śrī Vaiṣṇava context without attributing every later liturgical doctrine to one author. It also avoids masculine English pronouns as proof that brahman is simply a human-like male individual enlarged to cosmic scale.'
  ]),
  section('path','Action, knowledge, bhakti, surrender, and grace',[
    'Liberation requires more than assent to a metaphysical proposition. Karma-yoga disciplines action and detachment; knowledge clarifies the self’s dependence; sustained upāsanā or bhakti becomes loving meditation on brahman; divine grace brings the path to completion. Rāmānuja can describe bhakti as a form of direct, continuous knowing rather than emotion added after philosophy. The liberated self does not disappear. It enjoys unobstructed knowledge and loving service while remaining dependent on God, so personal relation is the fulfillment rather than the residue of an incomplete insight.',
    'Prapatti, surrender or taking refuge, became a major Śrī Vaiṣṇava path and a central point in later interpretation. Some accounts ground it strongly in Rāmānuja’s devotional prose; others emphasize the disciplined bhakti path of his systematic texts. The later Vaṭakalai and Teṉkalai traditions debate grace, effort, eligibility, Śrī, and textual authority through more than the famous monkey-and-cat analogies. Their differences should not be retrojected as two fully formed camps during Rāmānuja’s life, nor should prapatti be romanticized as erasing every historical boundary of caste and gender.'
  ],[],['Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya','Gadyatraya']),
  section('ethics-access','Duty, moral realism, and contested social access',[
    'Because persons, qualities, and values are real, ethical action is not merely a provisional ladder discarded as false. Duties can purify intention, express service, and order life toward God. Rāmānuja criticizes action pursued only for private reward and connects devotion with non-possessive performance. Yet his texts work within Brahmanical assumptions about varṇa, life stages, Vedic study, and ritual eligibility. The Oxford overview notes that the demanding bhakti path in his major Vedānta works is tied to twice-born male access, while later tradition presents surrender as more widely open.',
    'Hagiographies remember Rāmānuja as crossing caste boundaries, honoring non-Brahmin devotees, sharing teachings, and reforming institutions. These stories have inspired genuine projects of inclusion, but “social reformer” is also a modern biographical construction that needs evidence claim by claim. Respecting devotional memory does not require certifying a modern egalitarian program, and identifying restrictions does not make community interpretations irrelevant. The productive question is how metaphysical dependence, divine accessibility, teacher authority, temple participation, and inherited hierarchy have been negotiated across different periods.'
  ]),
  section('debate-reception','A living tradition shaped after Rāmānuja',[
    'Rāmānuja argues against Advaita and Bhedābheda, but his opponents are often presented as constructed prior positions rather than named contemporary individuals. He also engages Mīmāṃsā, Sāṃkhya, Nyāya, Vaiśeṣika, Buddhist views, and Pāñcarātra authority. His realism is not simply “common sense”: it is a developed account of intentional consciousness, substances and attributes, scripture, moral value, causation, and divine embodiment. Madhva later preserves real difference while rejecting Rāmānuja’s qualified unity, so theistic Vedānta contains irreducible philosophical disagreement.',
    'Successors including Sudarśana Sūri, Piḷḷai Lokācārya, Vedānta Deśika, and Maṇavāḷa Māmuni develop commentary, Tamil/Sanskrit pedagogy, surrender, ritual, and sectarian identity. The later northern/southern division is neither a trivial quarrel nor a complete map of every Śrī Vaiṣṇava community. Rāmānuja also influences other Vaiṣṇava Vedāntas and modern comparative theology. Reception confirms his generative power, not that every later doctrine was already fixed in his corpus. The page therefore makes successor disagreement visible instead of ending with an undifferentiated claim of influence.'
  ],['shankara','madhva','vedanta-desika']),
  section('reading-path','Reading a scholastic and devotional synthesis',[
    'Begin with Upaniṣadic and Bhagavad Gītā passages used by several Vedānta schools. Read the Vedārthasaṅgraha next because it displays Rāmānuja’s synthetic interpretation without requiring continuous navigation of the Brahma Sūtra. Then study the Śrī Bhāṣya’s opening debate, especially the great Advaita prior position and reply, with a guide that marks where the translation’s antique vocabulary misleads. Track three questions: what a self is, how a qualified thing is known, and how body–self dependence preserves both unity and distinction.',
    'Add the Gītā commentary and selected devotional prose to understand bhakti, surrender, and grace, but keep genre and authorship questions visible. Bartley provides a focused philosophical reconstruction; Ganeri maps scholarship and Śrī Vaiṣṇava contexts; Dutta disciplines biographical claims. Finally compare one shared sūtra in Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja, and Madhva. Do not ask which comes closest to a generic monotheism or monism. Ask what each commentator must explain about language, experience, plurality, divine perfection, action, and liberation—and what social practices make each explanation a lived tradition. Revisit the body–self analogy after that comparison: its philosophical precision becomes clearest when neither “identity” nor “difference” is allowed to answer every question in advance. Then examine one later Śrī Vaiṣṇava dispute in both Sanskrit and Tamil-oriented scholarship. That final step shows how a system remains alive through interpretation, ritual, memory, and contested authority rather than simply preserving a founder’s propositions.'
  ],['shankara','madhva'],['Vedārthasaṅgraha','Śrī Bhāṣya','Bhagavad Gītā Bhāṣya'])
];

const madhvaSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation','Difference, dependence, and a real world',[
    'Madhva is the principal early architect of Dvaita Vedānta, a tradition that makes real difference and complete dependence on Viṣṇu the starting points of philosophy. “Dualism” is convenient but incomplete: Madhva does not teach only two substances, and his ontology contains God, innumerable selves, matter, time, space, scripture, and other dependent realities. The decisive division is between the one independent reality, svatantra, and everything dependent, paratantra. Difference does not threaten divine supremacy because no finite being has independent existence, power, knowledge, or fulfillment.',
    'This position is a rival interpretation of Vedānta, not a refusal to understand nonduality. Madhva comments on the Upaniṣads, Bhagavad Gītā, and Brahma Sūtra and argues that their unity statements must be read alongside their many assertions of distinction, agency, devotion, and divine qualities. He rejects both Śaṅkara’s ultimate non-difference and Rāmānuja’s body–self unity. The world is real, selves remain distinct, and liberation perfects knowledge and devotion without converting anyone into God. His philosophy therefore tests whether dependence requires identity or can instead make eternal relation intelligible.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja'],['Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya','Anuvyākhyāna'],['vedanta','philosophy-of-religion','metaphysics']),
  section('life-hagiography','A remembered incarnation and a difficult chronology',[
    'Mādhva tradition remembers the teacher also called Ānandatīrtha and Pūrṇaprajña as an incarnation of Vāyu, born near Uḍupi, initiated by Acyutaprekṣa, victorious in debate, twice a traveler to Badarī, discoverer of a Kṛṣṇa image, organizer of worship, and an author who finally disappeared rather than died. The Sumadhvavijaya, composed by Nārāyaṇa Paṇḍitācārya within the early community, is the chief sacred biography. It is invaluable for ideals, places, opponents, disciples, and remembered charisma, but divinity and miracle are claims of the tradition rather than ordinary documentary facts.',
    'Chronology is disputed. The widely printed 1238–1317 dates reflect one reckoning; 1199–1278 and other ranges appear in scholarship and lineage calculation. The Atlas uses 1238–1317 as a conventional display with low confidence, not as precision certified by contemporary records. Historical reconstruction can locate Madhva in medieval coastal Karnataka and identify early disciples, works, institutions, and polemical contexts while leaving travel details and royal encounters open. Respect for hagiography is strongest when its genre is named instead of quietly turning sacred geography into a modern itinerary.'
  ]),
  section('corpus','A prolific corpus before later systematization',[
    'Tradition assigns Madhva thirty-seven works, often called the Sarvamūlagranthas. They include a concise Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya, the metrical Anuvyākhyāna and related sūtra works, commentaries on ten Upaniṣads and the Bhagavad Gītā, writings on the Mahābhārata and Bhāgavata Purāṇa, hymns, ritual texts, and short doctrinal treatises. The corpus is unusually compressed and allusive. It quotes texts and textual forms whose histories require investigation, uses etymology aggressively, and often assumes a debate or inherited teaching rather than pausing to build a modern systematic sequence.',
    'Jonathan Peterson cautions against reading the whole later Mādhva system directly out of one founder’s apparently chaotic and prolific archive. Jayatīrtha and subsequent commentators organize, defend, and sometimes redirect its arguments. That does not make Madhva a merely charismatic precursor: his works establish the independent/dependent framework, real difference, Viṣṇu’s supremacy, interpretive methods, and a durable polemical agenda. It does mean that a doctrine should be attributed to the named text or later author when possible. “Madhva says” cannot cover every refinement found in Vyāsatīrtha or a contemporary manual.'
  ],[],['Sarvamūlagranthas','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya','Anuvyākhyāna','Mahābhāratatātparyanirṇaya']),
  section('independent-dependent','Viṣṇu alone is independent',[
    'Madhva identifies the supreme brahman with Viṣṇu, who alone exists through himself and possesses unlimited knowledge, power, bliss, and perfection. Everything else is real because it depends on God, not because it shares God’s independent mode of being. Lakṣmī has a uniquely exalted and eternally liberated status but remains dependent; other deities occupy a graded cosmos under Viṣṇu. This hierarchy differs from generic monotheism because Vedic deities, scriptural names, cosmic offices, and forms of worship are interpreted within a specific Vaiṣṇava order rather than discarded as false gods.',
    'Dependence is ontological, epistemic, and practical. A finite self does not create its capacities, guarantee the objects it knows, control every result, or become the source of liberation. Yet dependence does not erase agency. Selves know, desire, act, accrue karma, misunderstand, and receive grace as real agents whose powers are sustained by God. Madhva must therefore coordinate divine governance with moral responsibility. His answer does not resemble modern libertarian freedom, and the tension remains philosophically live: if all capacity is dependent and souls differ intrinsically, what exactly makes praise, blame, and destiny just?'
  ]),
  section('five-differences','The five differences are not temporary mistakes',[
    'The later formula pañcabheda names five real differences: between God and individual selves, God and matter, one self and another, one material thing and another, and selves and matter. These distinctions are not products of ignorance scheduled to disappear. They make knowledge, language, action, devotion, and liberation possible. A knower must differ from an object known; a worshiper must differ from the one worshiped; individual lives must differ for karma and memory to belong to anyone. Difference is positive structure, not merely the absence of identity.',
    'Madhva nevertheless rejects a universe of unrelated atoms. Every dependent reality is ordered through Viṣṇu, and relations are themselves real. Two selves can share dependence, embodiment, or devotion while remaining irreducibly distinct. Material objects undergo genuine change, and the world has a public order available to perception and inference. This combination explains why “pluralistic realism” sometimes communicates better than “dualism.” The danger is to make it sound egalitarian: Madhva’s plurality is graded, not flat, and his account of intrinsic difference extends into controversial rankings among souls and divine beings.'
  ]),
  section('knowledge','Perception, inference, and reliable scripture',[
    'Mādhva epistemology recognizes perception, inference, and verbal testimony as principal means of knowledge. Perception discloses real objects rather than representations sealed inside a mind; inference extends knowledge through dependable relations; testimony communicates matters beyond ordinary access. The Veda is unauthored and authoritative, while other texts receive authority in a hierarchy of dependence and consistency. Madhva’s realism is therefore argued through ordinary cognition as well as scripture. If experience normally reveals difference, a nondual interpreter bears the burden of showing why that structure is finally canceled.',
    'Error does not prove that all cognition needs certification by an infinite regress of later cognitions. Mādhva thinkers develop accounts of cognition’s prima facie validity, correction, and the role of a witnessing subject, although detailed formulations belong partly to commentators. Scripture cannot license contradiction simply by being sacred: grammar, context, cross-textual coherence, and established means of knowledge constrain interpretation. Conversely, inference cannot independently settle the supersensible nature of God or liberation. The schools disagree not about reason versus faith, but about how testimony and public reasoning cooperate, what each can reach, and when one reading defeats another.'
  ],[],['Viṣṇutattvanirṇaya']),
  section('language-hermeneutics','Reading the whole canon for divine supremacy',[
    'Madhva’s commentarial practice assumes that the Vedic archive ultimately teaches Viṣṇu’s supremacy and real difference. He reads divine names expansively, uses etymological analysis or nirukti, coordinates epic and Purāṇic materials with the prasthānatrayī, and interprets apparently nondual sentences through dependence, similarity, indwelling control, or contextual restriction. “That you are” cannot make the finite hearer absolutely identical with the omniscient Lord because direct experience, other passages, and the attributes of each would be contradicted. Unity instead marks an intimate relation grounded in God.',
    'Some famous reports reduce his interpretation to inserting an extra negative sound into tat tvam asi. That anecdotal shorthand is a poor substitute for his wider hermeneutics and the manuscript questions surrounding particular readings. His method is also contested: opponents charge that unusual etymologies and otherwise unattested sources make the canon say what the system already requires. Defenders locate those moves within inherited Vedic semantic practices and a broader hierarchy of texts. A fair account neither treats Madhva’s exegesis as self-evident literalism nor assumes that Advaita alone offers sophisticated interpretation.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja'],['Chāndogya Upaniṣad 6.8.7']),
  section('self-liberation','Distinct selves, devotion, grace, and liberation',[
    'A self is an enduring, conscious, embodied-under-bondage agent that never becomes identical with Viṣṇu or another self. Ignorance concerns God, one’s own dependent nature, and the structure of reality; liberating knowledge corrects it, but knowledge is inseparable from devotion. Bhakti is lucid recognition, love, worship, and service directed toward the supremely worthy Lord. Ethical discipline and study prepare the person, while divine grace is indispensable. Liberation removes suffering and obscuration and brings a fitting vision and enjoyment of God without abolishing the devotee.',
    'Because individuality endures, liberated experience can differ in degree without becoming deficient in the ordinary sense: each self realizes a fulfillment appropriate to its intrinsic capacity. This claim gives eternal relationship unusual metaphysical force, but it also ties salvation to hierarchy. A modern reader should resist translating mokṣa into either private happiness or endless personality as presently experienced. Madhva describes transformed knowledge and bliss within an ordered community of dependent selves. The philosophical issue is whether perfect dependence and differentiated fulfillment preserve relational value or make inequality ultimate.'
  ]),
  section('hierarchy-destiny','Tāratamya and the disturbing doctrine of unequal destinies',[
    'Madhva teaches tāratamya, a graded order among divine beings and selves, and the tradition distinguishes souls eligible for liberation, souls subject to continuing transmigration, and souls destined for darkness. Some accounts add eternally damned beings and cite scriptural classifications to defend the view. This is not a small decorative feature. It bears on agency, divine justice, grace, and the meaning of intrinsic nature. It also prevents an easy presentation of Dvaita as simply a warm defense of personal individuality against impersonal monism.',
    'Interpretation requires precision. Later Mādhva texts systematize vocabulary and consequences, translations vary, and comparison with Christian predestination or hell can clarify only if differences in karma, beginningless selves, rebirth, and divine dependence remain visible. Critics ask how a good God can sustain irreducibly unequal capacities and destinies. Defenders argue that justice assigns results without making all beings interchangeable and that bliss is complete relative to capacity. The Atlas presents the doctrine and objection together; suppressing it would be apologetics, while making it the only thing worth knowing would caricature a much larger tradition.'
  ]),
  section('polemics','Debate with Advaita and other Indian philosophies',[
    'Madhva targets Advaita’s account of a world that is ultimately sublated, an identity between self and brahman, and an ignorance difficult to locate in nondual reality. If difference is false, he asks, how can scripture, teacher, student, error, and liberating cognition retain enough validity to remove it? He also rejects Rāmānuja’s claim that selves and matter form God’s body or modes, since dependence does not require inclusion within one qualified substance. These are competing explanations of common texts and experience, not three stages toward a compromise.',
    'His corpus also argues with Buddhist, Jain, Mīmāṃsā, Nyāya, Sāṃkhya, and other Vedānta positions. Polemical labels may compress diverse opponents, and claims of debate victory belong partly to literary genre. Later authors sharpen the technical encounter: Jayatīrtha clarifies Madhva’s arguments; Vyāsatīrtha’s Nyāyāmṛta presses a formidable realist critique of Advaita; Madhusūdana Sarasvatī responds in the Advaitasiddhi. Following that exchange reveals a shared scholastic language of inference, absence, error, and scripture beneath real theological conflict.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja'],['Nyāyāmṛta','Advaitasiddhi']),
  section('transmission','From Uḍupi lineages to early modern realism',[
    'Early Mādhva identity formed through works, sacred biographies, monasteries, worship, pilgrimage places, teacher lineages, and disputes over authority. Uḍupi’s Kṛṣṇa temple and the eight maṭhas are central living institutions, but their received histories should not all be projected unchanged into Madhva’s lifetime. Padmanābha Tīrtha, Narahari Tīrtha, Akṣobhya Tīrtha, and especially Jayatīrtha helped transmit and interpret the corpus. “Founder and faithful followers” misses how commentary makes a tradition intellectually usable.',
    'In the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries Vyāsatīrtha developed Mādhva realism in major debates and served within the Vijayanagara world. Later teachers, polemicists, Haridāsa poets, and regional communities connected scholastic doctrine with Kannada devotion and performance in varied ways; Purandara Dāsa and Kanaka Dāsa should not be reduced to illustrations of Sanskrit metaphysics. Modern Dvaita scholarship, diaspora institutions, and online teaching extend the history again. Madhva’s legacy is thus a layered ecology of argument, worship, language, memory, and institutional succession rather than one frozen list of five differences.'
  ],[],['Nyāyāmṛta']),
  section('reading-path','A reading path through argument and lineage',[
    'Begin with Stoker’s concise overview and Sarma’s longer introduction to learn the independent/dependent distinction before entering Madhva’s compressed prose and verse. Read translated selections from the Viṣṇutattvanirṇaya, Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya, and Anuvyākhyāna with the source passage beside them. Track every use of “difference”: does it name perceptual structure, numerical distinction, dependence, scriptural purport, or hierarchy? Then compare a shared Upaniṣadic sentence or Brahma Sūtra in Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja rather than relying on a textbook chart.',
    'Next read the Sumadhvavijaya as sacred biography while marking miracle, geography, succession, and polemic by genre. Peterson supplies the early institutional caution; Williams follows later realist argument through Vyāsatīrtha. Finish with a living Dvaita source and a historically specific Haridāsa work, without assuming either merely translates Madhva. Keep founder, early commentators, early-modern system, devotional culture, and modern reception in separate columns. That method lets doctrinal continuity remain visible while preventing later clarity from becoming imaginary thirteenth-century documentation. Return at the end to soul hierarchy and grace. A reading that can celebrate realism but cannot face differentiated destiny has not yet encountered the system’s full philosophical cost.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja'],['Viṣṇutattvanirṇaya','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya','Anuvyākhyāna','Sumadhvavijaya'])
];

const vedantaSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation','A family of rival readings, not one doctrine',[
    'Vedānta names a large family of South Asian philosophical and religious traditions organized around interpreting the Upaniṣads and, in influential later formulations, the Bhagavad Gītā and Brahma Sūtra. Its thinkers ask what brahman is, what kind of self knows and acts, whether the world is ultimately real, how language reaches what exceeds ordinary objects, and what knowledge, action, devotion, discipline, surrender, or grace can accomplish. They do not answer in one voice. Advaita, Viśiṣṭādvaita, Dvaita, Bhedābheda, Śuddhādvaita, and other formations defend incompatible metaphysics and paths.',
    'The familiar translation “end of the Veda” can indicate both the textual location of Upaniṣadic materials and a claimed culmination or purpose of Vedic teaching. Neither sense gives a single historical founding date. Vedānta did not begin with Śaṅkara, and it is not identical with his Advaita. The Atlas therefore treats approximately 800 BCE as a broad Upaniṣadic horizon, not the birthday of a finished school. “Classical Vedānta” is itself a retrospective category whose boundaries expand or contract with corpus, lineage, language, institution, and scholarly purpose.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja','madhva'],['Upaniṣads','Bhagavad Gītā','Brahma Sūtra'],['indian-philosophy','metaphysics','philosophy-of-religion']),
  section('layered-archive','The layered archive before the schools',[
    'The Upaniṣads are not one book or a uniform creed. Composed and transmitted across long periods and settings, they preserve dialogue, ritual speculation, cosmology, instruction, poetry, and argument about self, breath, death, desire, knowledge, and ultimate reality. Statements of identity sit beside accounts of creation, inner control, divine persons, plurality, and negation. Later Vedāntins select, rank, and reconcile these voices through inherited hermeneutics. A school’s elegant synthesis should not be mistaken for the archive simply announcing that synthesis in advance.',
    'The Bhagavad Gītā places knowledge, disciplined action, devotion, duty, divine manifestation, and liberation in a dramatic conversation and becomes central to many, though not every, Vedānta lineage. The compressed Brahma Sūtra organizes disputes about Upaniṣadic purport, competing explanations of the world, the self, practice, and release. Its authorship, date, and early interpretive history are not securely reduced to one person named Bādarāyaṇa. Because aphorisms are terse, commentary does constitutive philosophical work: it supplies opponents, connections, grammar, and the very problem a line is taken to solve.'
  ]),
  section('early-plurality','Vedānta before the famous three',[
    'Doxographies and later commentaries preserve traces of earlier Vedānta interpreters such as Aśmarathya, Audulomi, Kāśakṛtsna, Bādarī, Bhartṛprapañca, Brahmadatta, and others whose works are lost or fragmentary. Gauḍapāda’s kārikās became crucial for Advaita, while Maṇḍana Miśra developed a powerful form of nondual Vedānta not identical in every respect with Śaṅkara. These figures make a simple sequence—Upaniṣads, then Śaṅkara—historically impossible. Arguments about difference, causation, knowledge, action, and liberation were already underway.',
    'Bhedābheda, difference-and-nondifference, is especially important. Bhāskara, Yādavaprakāśa, Nimbārka traditions, and other thinkers explain how brahman and the world can be neither simply identical nor utterly separate, though their models differ. Nicholson shows that such positions may have been highly prominent before later surveys centered Advaita. “Difference-and-nondifference” is not one compromise halfway between monism and dualism. It names a family of causal, relational, and theological proposals that continued alongside the better-known commentarial schools.'
  ],[],['Māṇḍūkya Kārikā']),
  section('commentary-method','Metaphysics made through commentary',[
    'Vedānta systems emerge by reading a shared but internally varied textual inheritance. Commentators ask which passages state the main purport, which prepare a learner, which speak figuratively, how negation functions, whether identity is literal or relational, and how apparently conflicting statements can be coordinated. They use grammar, etymology, contextual sequence, Mīmāṃsā rules of interpretation, inference, perception, inherited commentary, and sectarian scripture. The result is not a detachable metaphysics with quotations pasted on; exegetical method helps determine what kinds of entities and relations can be affirmed.',
    'The same sentence can therefore anchor opposed conclusions. Śaṅkara reads “that you are” as disclosing non-difference once limiting predicates are removed. Rāmānuja reads coordinated reference to brahman qualified by a real dependent self. Madhva reads unity through dependence and other contextual relations while preserving eternal difference. Calling one “mystical,” another “theistic,” and the third “literal” avoids the actual arguments. A comparative exhibit must place interpretive rule, account of knowledge, and metaphysical consequence together, then let each school state what it thinks the rivals cannot explain.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja','madhva'],['Chāndogya Upaniṣad 6.8.7']),
  section('advaita','Advaita: nonduality and the removal of misidentification',[
    'Advaita holds that brahman is nondual consciousness and that the deepest self is not ultimately different from it. Śaṅkara explains bondage through superimposition of body, mind, agency, and limitation upon self; liberating knowledge removes the error rather than producing a new eternal object. The experienced world remains transactionally valid while it is understood, yet lacks independent ultimate status. Later Advaitins debate ignorance’s locus and object, the role of reflection and contemplation, how one consciousness appears as many, and how precisely to describe empirical reality.',
    'This is not the claim that an individual ego imagines the universe, nor does māyā translate adequately as “nothing exists.” Worship, action, ethical discipline, testimony, and teachers operate within the valid order where instruction occurs. At the same time, Advaita gives liberating knowledge a direct role that ritual action cannot share as a co-producer. Its rivals ask whether an unexplained ignorance can appear in nondual consciousness and whether a scripture embedded in the empirical order can cancel that order. Those objections are part of Advaita’s history, not misunderstandings outside it.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja','madhva'],['Upadeśasāhasrī','Brahma Sūtra Bhāṣya']),
  section('visistadvaita','Viśiṣṭādvaita: one differentiated divine reality',[
    'Rāmānuja’s Viśiṣṭādvaita identifies brahman with Nārāyaṇa, endowed with unlimited auspicious qualities and inseparable from real selves and matter. The world and souls form the body of brahman: they are supported, controlled, and ordered toward the divine while retaining their own characteristics. Nonduality means that no reality is independent of this inclusive whole, not that difference disappears. Consciousness belongs to enduring knowers; bondage constricts the self’s knowing; liberation restores unobstructed knowledge and loving service without dissolving personal identity.',
    'The school’s Śrī Vaiṣṇava life joins Sanskrit Vedānta to Tamil Āḻvār poetry, temple worship, Pāñcarātra traditions, teacher lineages, and later debates about surrender and grace. Those dimensions cannot all be credited to one author or compressed into “qualified monism.” Rāmānuja’s major systematic works give disciplined bhakti a demanding place, while prapatti becomes central across later community interpretations. Advaita critics of qualification and Dvaita critics of body–self unity expose the genuine pressure in describing real plurality as both God’s body and inseparable from divine perfection.'
  ],['ramanuja','shankara','madhva'],['Śrī Bhāṣya','Vedārthasaṅgraha']),
  section('dvaita','Dvaita: real plurality under one independent Lord',[
    'Madhva’s Dvaita makes Viṣṇu the sole independent reality and everything else genuinely real but completely dependent. God, souls, and matter never collapse into one substance, and five kinds of difference organize knowing, action, and worship. Liberation perfects a self’s knowledge, bliss, and devotion while preserving its distinct identity. This is more than a two-reality dualism: it is a graded pluralism ordered through divine sovereignty. The world’s reality and cognition’s ordinary reliability provide resources for a sustained critique of Advaita.',
    'Dvaita also introduces difficult claims about intrinsic hierarchy among selves and differentiated destinies. These cannot be removed to make the tradition fit a modern celebration of individuality. Nor should they eclipse its epistemology, hermeneutics, devotional practice, and powerful later scholasticism. Jayatīrtha and Vyāsatīrtha systematize and defend positions that should not automatically be projected into every line of Madhva. Uḍupi institutions and Kannada Haridāsa traditions add further histories that overlap with, but are not reducible to, Sanskrit polemic.'
  ],['madhva','shankara','ramanuja'],['Anuvyākhyāna','Nyāyāmṛta']),
  section('other-vedantas','Other Vaiṣṇava and Śaiva Vedāntas',[
    'The three-school survey leaves much of Vedānta outside the room. Nimbārka traditions articulate forms of difference-and-nondifference; Vallabha’s Śuddhādvaita affirms a pure nondual brahman identified with Kṛṣṇa while treating the world as a real manifestation; Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇava theologians develop acintya-bhedābheda, inconceivable difference and nondifference; Śrīkaṇṭha and later Appayya Dīkṣita interpret the Brahma Sūtra through Śaiva commitments. Each label contains commentarial and institutional history, not just a formula placed between three poles.',
    'Other Vedāntins work through devotional communities, vernacular literatures, ritual systems, regional institutions, and debates whose primary archive exceeds the prasthānatrayī. Even schools sharing devotion to Viṣṇu disagree about divine form, causation, the status of selves, grace, and liberation. “Vedānta says God is personal” is therefore no better than “Vedānta says only consciousness is real.” A museum map should make absence visible: a concise Atlas cannot represent every lineage, but it can prevent the selected trio from masquerading as a complete taxonomy.'
  ]),
  section('shared-questions','Shared questions without a common answer',[
    'Across schools, brahman, ātman, world, bondage, and mokṣa recur, but shared vocabulary can conceal opposed reference. Is brahman consciousness without differentiating characteristics, a personal whole qualified by real souls and matter, or the supreme Lord eternally distinct from dependent beings? Is a self pure consciousness, a knower whose awareness expands and contracts, or a permanently distinct agent? Is causation manifestation, real transformation, dependent reorganization, or appearance? A comparison must answer these questions in full sentences rather than awarding every school the same keyword badge.',
    'Epistemology also matters. Vedāntins debate perception, inference, testimony, error, memory, intrinsic or extrinsic validity, and who is eligible to receive particular teachings. Scripture is usually authoritative, but its scope and relation to reason are argued. Experience is invoked, yet “experience” may mean ordinary cognition, contemplative realization, remembered devotion, or a modern category of private religion. No neutral mystical encounter sits underneath the systems waiting to be translated three ways. Claims about realization are formed by disciplines, concepts, texts, teachers, bodies, and communities.'
  ]),
  section('practice-social-world','Paths, institutions, and unequal access',[
    'Vedānta is practiced through study, commentary, listening, reasoning, meditation, renunciation, ritual action, temple worship, domestic devotion, recitation, pilgrimage, initiation, surrender, service, and teacher–student relations in combinations that differ by lineage. Knowledge and devotion are not always competing paths: bhakti can be a form of knowing, while Advaita devotion can prepare and orient a learner. Grace can complete disciplined practice without making practice meaningless. The modern opposition between “intellectual philosophy” and “popular religion” distorts communities in which exegesis, embodiment, liturgy, and institutional authority interact.',
    'Those institutions also regulate access. Classical works often assume varṇa, gender, life-stage, and Vedic eligibility distinctions; renouncer and temple lineages define authority; hagiographies remember both boundary enforcement and boundary crossing. Modern teachers sometimes universalize teachings through print, English, public lectures, and digital media while retaining or revising inherited hierarchies. Metaphysical unity, divine embodiment, or devotional equality does not by itself prove social equality. Responsible reception asks who could study, worship, teach, own resources, and interpret—and how different communities contested those permissions.'
  ]),
  section('modern-reception','Modern Vedānta, Hindu unity, and global translation',[
    'Colonial scholarship, missionary critique, print culture, reform movements, nationalism, comparative religion, and new institutions reshaped the category “Vedānta.” Rammohun Roy, Vivekananda, Ramakrishna movements, Aurobindo, Radhakrishnan, Ramana Maharshi, and many others offered distinct modern constructions rather than one Neo-Vedānta. Some elevated Advaita as the philosophical core of Hinduism, reconciled religions through a higher unity, joined service to spirituality, or spoke in vocabularies of experience and universal consciousness. These projects answered modern pressures and created new possibilities; they were not simple repetitions of Śaṅkara.',
    'Nicholson traces precolonial efforts to classify and unify Indian systems, preventing the opposite error that Hindu unity was invented from nothing by Europeans. Still, earlier doxography, colonial “Hinduism,” nationalist appropriation, and global wellness are different historical processes. Contemporary neuroscience comparisons, nondual therapy, perennialism, and consciousness discourse can stimulate philosophy, but they must identify which Vedānta, whose concept of awareness, and what has changed in translation. “All religions teach Vedānta” is a contested modern claim, not a neutral description of classical disagreement.'
  ]),
  section('method-misunderstanding','How to compare without building a winner’s podium',[
    'Textbook tables often place Advaita, Viśiṣṭādvaita, and Dvaita on a line from monism to dualism. The table can orient, but it encourages a winner’s podium in which one school looks most sophisticated and the others become compromises or devotional concessions. Better comparison follows one problem across sources: how does each read a sūtra, explain error, preserve divine perfection, account for agency, and describe liberation? It also identifies dates, genre, later commentary, and community practice rather than treating named founders as simultaneous participants in one debate.',
    'Common corrections follow. Vedānta is not all Indian philosophy; Hindu traditions are not all Vedānta; Advaita is not every form of Vedānta; nonduality is not egoic omnipotence; Dvaita is not generic Western theism; Viśiṣṭādvaita is not merely a midpoint; bhakti is not philosophy’s emotional afterthought; and Bhedābheda is not an indecisive compromise. Diacritics and English translations help orientation but never settle interpretation. Most importantly, shared scriptures do not guarantee a hidden consensus: traditions can inhabit one archive while disagreeing about reality itself.'
  ]),
  section('reading-path','A comparative reading path through the archive',[
    'Begin with several Upaniṣadic dialogues rather than an anthology of identity slogans. Add the Bhagavad Gītā while marking action, knowledge, devotion, duty, divine form, and liberation. Read a historical guide to the Brahma Sūtra before its commentaries, because a bare aphorism rarely reveals the dispute it later organizes. Then select one passage—such as Brahma Sūtra 1.1.1 or Chāndogya Upaniṣad 6.8.7—and compare Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja, and Madhva with modern notes. Record each commentator’s rule of interpretation, ontology, epistemic standard, and practical consequence.',
    'Next break the trio. Read Nicholson on Bhedābheda and the historical formation of system categories; sample Bhāskara, Nimbārka, Vallabha, Gauḍīya, or Śaiva Vedānta through specialist scholarship; then study one living lineage in its regional and institutional setting. Finish with a modern interpreter while keeping a two-column ledger: classical source and modern reconstruction. The goal is not to discover which school secretly says what a contemporary reader already believes. It is to understand how sustained, accountable disagreement can turn a layered archive into multiple philosophical worlds.'
  ],['shankara','ramanuja','madhva'],['Upaniṣads','Bhagavad Gītā','Brahma Sūtra','Upadeśasāhasrī','Vedārthasaṅgraha'])
];

